meta {
  name: Login Unknown Email (401)
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "nobody-has-this-address@example.com",
    "password": "definitely-the-wrong-password"
  }
}

docs {
  An account that does not exist. Expect 401 with a body BYTE-IDENTICAL to
  Wrong Password (401).

  This is the user-enumeration check, and it has two halves:

  1. The body must match seq 9 exactly. Different wording for "no such user"
     hands an attacker a list of registered addresses.

  2. The ELAPSED TIME must be comparable to seq 9 — same order of magnitude,
     within roughly 1.5x. A miss that returns markedly faster than a hit
     leaks the same information through a side channel.

  If seq 10 is much faster than seq 9, burnTimingBudget() in lib/passwords.ts
  is not being called on the miss path. Run each a few times: a single sample
  is noisy, and the first request after an idle Neon database pays an extra
  ~500ms of wake-up time that has nothing to do with argon2.
}
